=== Homework Policy

Students are encouraged to discuss homework problems in groups. However, each assignment must be completed and submitted individually unless it is explicitly designated as a group assignment. Homework plagiarism will not be tolerated.

Each student has two free opportunities to submit a homework assignment late during the semester. Each late assignment must be submitted within three days after its original deadline. Homework submitted beyond the three-day grace period will not be graded. Students with special circumstances should contact the instructor.

=== Reference Texts

The main study materials for this course will be the lecture slides; there is no mandatory textbook, but the following references are recommended for the respective topics.

- Deisenroth, Marc Peter, A. Aldo Faisal, and Cheng Soon Ong. /Mathematics for Machine Learning/. Cambridge University Press. [https://mml-book.github.io/ Book website].
- Arora, Raman, et al. /Theory of Deep Learning/ (2020). [https://www.cs.princeton.edu/courses/archive/fall19/cos597B/lecnotes/bookdraft.pdf Available online].
- Ma, Wing-Kin. /ENGG 5781: Matrix Analysis and Computations/. Department of Electronic Engineering, The Chinese University of Hong Kong. [https://www.ee.cuhk.edu.hk/~wkma/engg5781/ Course materials].
- Hastie, Trevor, Robert Tibshirani, and Jerome Friedman. /The Elements of Statistical Learning/ (2009).
- Casella, George, and Roger Berger. /Statistical Inference/. CRC Press, 2024.
- Chewi, Sinho. /Log-Concave Sampling/ (2024). [https://chewisinho.github.io/main.pdf Available online].
- Norris, James R. /Markov Chains/. Cambridge University Press, 1998.
- Boyd, Stephen P., and Lieven Vandenberghe. /Convex Optimization/. Cambridge University Press, 2004.
- Stanford University. /EE364b: Lecture Slides and Notes/. [https://stanford.edu/class/ee364b/lectures.html Course materials].
- Cover, Thomas M. /Elements of Information Theory/. John Wiley & Sons, 1999.
- Yeung, Raymond W. /Information Theory and Network Coding/. Springer, 2008.

=== Evaluation and Feedback

- Formal Course and Teaching Evaluation
- Informal feedback to the instructor and teaching assistants
- Departmental retreat and program review

=== Academic Honesty and Use of AI

AI tools are not allowed for completing homework assignments unless their use is explicitly permitted by the instructor for a particular assignment. Students must complete all coursework and assessments independently unless AI-tool use is explicitly permitted. Unauthorized use of AI may constitute academic dishonesty and harm learning. Misuse includes submitting AI-generated work as one's own, using AI to cheat, or employing AI unethically. Students must obtain permission before using AI in academic work and acknowledge any permitted AI use in their submissions.

